{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-148349",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+1}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y+1}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y+1}-2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y+1} & =x+2 \\\\ {e}^{y+1} & =2x+4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
